from ./Errors import Errors
from ./Escapes import Escapes
from ./Layout import Layout

export anchor Values:
    description:
        Values are what follow the colon, both in a component's properties and
        in an instance's attributes. This section sets out how a value is
        split into parts, and which units and colour formats Emul accepts.

    propertyValues:
        A property's value is everything after the colon to the end of the
        line, with the spaces around it trimmed. A value can't continue onto
        the next line.

    splitting:
        Within a value, parts are separated as follows:

        - spaces
            Separate the parts of a single value, such as the four sides in
            `padding: 8px 12px 8px 12px`. Any run of spaces counts as one.
        - commas
            Separate whole items in properties that take a list, such as
            several shadows in `boxShadow`, or several typefaces in
            `fontFamily`.
        - slashes
            Separate the halves of properties whose entry says so, such as
            `aspectRatio: 16 / 9` or `gridColumn: 1 / 3`. Spaces around the
            slash are optional.
        - parentheses
            Group the arguments of a function, such as `rgba()` or
            `repeat()`. Commas and slashes inside parentheses belong to the
            function and do not split the value. Parentheses with no function
            name before them hold an arithmetic expression, as described
            under arithmetic.
        - quotes
            Group text that contains spaces, such as a typeface name like
            `"Helvetica Neue"`, or the row strings of `gridTemplateAreas`.
            Commas, slashes and parentheses inside quotes are just text.

    bodyEntries:
        In a composition, a body's entries are separated by commas or line
        breaks. Only a comma at the top level of the body ends an entry. A
        comma inside quotes or parentheses does not, so quoted text and
        quoted attribute values can contain commas freely. A comma in an
        unquoted attribute value must be escaped, as described in
        @{Escapes}.

    units:
        Sizes are given in exactly two units:

        - px
            Pixels, written as a number followed by `px`, such as `12px` or
            `0.5px`.
        - percent
            A percentage, written as a number followed by `%`, such as `50%`.
            What it is a percentage of is set by each property's entry.

        Wherever a property's entry lists `<length>`, it means a value in
        `px`. A percentage is only accepted where the entry also lists
        `<percentage>`. Other CSS units, such as `em`, `rem`, `vw` and `vh`,
        are not accepted.

        Zero may be written without a unit. Negative values are accepted only
        where a property's entry says so.

    otherUnits:
        A few values are not sizes, and have units of their own:

        - deg
            Angles, as in `rotate: 45deg`.
        - fr
            A share of the free space in a grid track, as in
            `gridTemplateColumns: 1fr 2fr`.
        - numbers
            Plain numbers with no unit, for properties such as `opacity`,
            `flexGrow`, `lineHeight` and `scale`.
        - integers
            Whole numbers, for properties such as `zIndex` and `lineClamp`.

    arithmetic:
        Values can be calculated with operators. A calculation is written
        inside parentheses with no function name before them, such as
        `padding: (8px * 2) (24px / 2)` or `width: (100% - 20%)`. Emul
        calculates each expression to one number, with its unit, before the
        property reads it. The property then checks the result exactly as if
        it had been written out, so `gap: (8px * 2)` is the same as
        `gap: 16px`, and a result that the property does not accept is an
        `invalid-value` error.

        An expression may appear wherever a single number or size may,
        including inside a function, such as
        `rgba((200 + 55), 0, 0, (1 - 0.5))`. It holds only numbers, with or
        without a unit, the truth values `true` and `false`, the operators
        described here, and further parentheses to group. Keywords such as
        `auto`, colours and functions can't be part of an expression.

    operators:
        The arithmetic operators work on numbers and sizes:

        - +
            Adds two values with the same unit.
        - -
            Subtracts the right value from the left. Both have the same unit.
        - *
            Multiplies two values. At least one of them is a plain number,
            and the result has the unit of the other.
        - /
            Divides the left value by the right. If the right value is a plain
            number, the result has the unit of the left. If both have the same
            unit, the result is a plain number, as in `(32px / 8px)`, which is
            `4`.
        - %
            The remainder after dividing the left value by the right, with
            the sign of the left value, so `(-7 % 3)` is `-1`. The right value
            is a plain number or has the unit of the left. The result has the
            unit of the left.

        The ternary operator, described under ternary, chooses between two
        values: `condition ? a : b`.

    comparisons:
        The comparison operators compare two numbers or sizes and give a
        truth value, `true` or `false`:

        - ==
            Equal.
        - !=
            Not equal.
        - <
            Less than.
        - <=
            Less than or equal.
        - >
            Greater than.
        - >=
            Greater than or equal.

        The two values must have the same unit, so `(8px < 50%)` and
        `(8px == 8)` are `incompatible-units` errors. Units are never
        converted. Numbers are compared as Emul writes them, rounded to four
        decimal places, so `(0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3)` is `true`.

        `==` and `!=` may also compare two truth values, as in
        `(true != false)`. The other comparison operators can't take a truth
        value, and a truth value is never equal to a number; both are
        `invalid-operand` errors. Since a comparison gives a truth value,
        comparisons can't be chained: `(1 < 2 < 3)` is an error, and is
        written `(1 < 2 && 2 < 3)` instead.

    truthValues:
        The truth values are written `true` and `false`. They come from the
        comparison and logical operators, or are written directly in an
        expression.

        Wherever a condition is read, by the logical operators and by the
        ternary operator, every value is either truthy or falsy:

        - falsy
            `false`, and zero with any unit or none, such as `0`, `0px` and
            `0%`.
        - truthy
            `true`, and every number that is not zero, including negative
            numbers and fractions, such as `-2px` and `0.5`.

        A property can't take a truth value. A truth value is used as the
        condition of the ternary operator, or as a value of the logical
        operators or of `==` and `!=`. An expression whose final result is a
        truth value, such as `gap: (4px > 2px)`, is a `boolean-result` error.
        The arithmetic operators can't take a truth value, so `(true + 1)` is
        an `invalid-operand` error.

    logicalOperators:
        The logical operators combine conditions. Each takes any values,
        reads them as truthy or falsy, and gives a truth value:

        - &&
            And. `true` when both values are truthy, as in
            `(4px > 2px && 1 < 2)`.
        - ||
            Or. `true` when either value is truthy, as in
            `(0px || 50%)`.
        - !
            Not. Written before a single value, `true` when that value is
            falsy, as in `(!false)` or `(!(1px > 2px))`.

        Both values of `&&` and `||` are always calculated, so an error in
        either one is reported even when the other decides the result.
        Single `&` and `|` are not operators, and are `invalid-expression`
        errors.

    ternary:
        The ternary operator `condition ? a : b` gives `a` when the condition
        is truthy and `b` when it is falsy, as in
        `gap: (4px > 2px ? 16px : 8px)`, which is `16px`. The condition is
        usually a comparison or a logical expression, but any value can be
        the condition, read as truthy or falsy as described under
        truthValues.

        Both `a` and `b` are full expressions, and they may have different
        units, since only one of them is the result. Both are calculated, so
        an error in either one is reported. A ternary groups from the right,
        so `(1 > 2 ? 1px : 2 > 1 ? 2px : 3px)` reads as
        `(1 > 2 ? 1px : (2 > 1 ? 2px : 3px))`, which is `2px`.
        A ternary may sit inside parentheses within a larger expression, as
        in `((1 < 2 ? 10px : 0px) * 2)`.

        The condition, the `?` and the `:` must all be present: a `?` with no
        condition before it, a `?` with no `:` after it, a `:` with no `?`
        before it, or a `?` or `:` with no value after it is an
        `invalid-condition` error.

    operatorSyntax:
        Each binary operator, and each `?` and `:`, has a space on each side,
        as in `(8px + 4px)` or `(4px > 2px ? 1 : 2)`. `!` is written
        directly before its value, or with spaces after it, and has a space
        or `(` before it. This keeps the operators apart from the rest of
        the value syntax:

        - slash separator
            A `/` is division only inside an expression's parentheses.
            Anywhere else, such as in `aspectRatio: 16 / 9`, it stays the
            separator described under splitting.
        - percentage unit
            A `%` written directly after a number, with no space, is the
            percentage unit, as in `50%`. A `%` with a space on each side, in
            an expression, is the remainder operator. So `(50% % 3)` is the
            remainder of `50%` divided by `3`.
        - negative numbers
            A `-` written directly before a number, with no space, is part of
            the number, as in `-4px`. A `-` with a space on each side is
            subtraction, so `(10px - -4px)` is `14px`.
        - colons
            A property's name ends at the first colon on its line, so the `:`
            of a ternary inside its value does not split the line.

    precedence:
        From first to last, an expression is calculated in this order.
        Operators on the same level are calculated from left to right, except
        the ternary operator, which groups from the right.

        - parentheses
            Inner parentheses are calculated before what is around them.
        - not
            `!`.
        - multiplication
            `*`, `/` and `%`.
        - addition
            `+` and `-`.
        - ordering
            `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=`.
        - equality
            `==` and `!=`.
        - and
            `&&`.
        - or
            `||`.
        - ternary
            `? :`.

        So `(2px + 3 * 4px)` is `14px`, `((2 + 3) * 4px)` is `20px`,
        `(2px + 2px == 2 * 2px)` is `true`, and
        `(true || false && false)` is `true`. Because `!` comes first,
        `(!1 == false)` reads as `((!1) == false)`, which is `true`.

    arithmeticErrors:
        The errors an expression can give are listed in @{Errors}:

        - invalid-expression
            The expression is malformed: an operator without the spaces it
            needs, an operator with no value before or after it, two values
            with no operator between them, empty or unclosed parentheses, a
            single `=`, `&` or `|`, or something other than a number, a
            truth value or an operator inside.
        - incompatible-units
            The units of the two values don't fit the operator, such as
            `(8px + 50%)`, `(8px - 2)`, `(8px * 2px)` or `(8px < 50%)`.
        - division-by-zero
            The right value of `/` or `%` is zero.
        - invalid-operand
            A truth value is used with an arithmetic operator or with `<`,
            `<=`, `>` or `>=`, or `==` or `!=` compares a truth value with a
            number.
        - invalid-condition
            A ternary is missing its condition, its `?`, its `:`, or a value
            after either of them.
        - boolean-result
            The final result of an expression is a truth value, which no
            property takes.

    colours:
        Wherever a property's entry lists `<color>`, the value is written in
        one of these four formats. Each is written exactly as CSS allows,
        including both the comma-separated and the space-separated forms of
        the functions, and out-of-range values are clamped as CSS clamps
        them.

        - hex
            A `#` followed by three, four, six or eight hexadecimal digits,
            such as `#fff`, `#ffff`, `#ffffff` or `#ffffff80`. The fourth or
            last pair of digits is the opacity.
        - rgba
            `rgba(red, green, blue, alpha)`, with each colour channel from 0
            to 255 or as a percentage, and alpha from 0 to 1 or as a
            percentage. `rgb()` is accepted as the same function, as in
            CSS.
        - hsl
            `hsl(hue, saturation, lightness)`, with hue in degrees and the
            other two as percentages, and an optional alpha. `hsla()` is
            accepted as the same function, as in CSS.
        - oklch
            `oklch(lightness chroma hue)`, written as in CSS, with an
            optional alpha after a slash.

        Named colours, such as `red`, and keywords such as `transparent` and
        `currentColor` are not accepted. A fully transparent colour is written
        as `#0000`.

    images:
        Wherever a property's entry lists an image, the value is
        `url(<path>)`, or a `linear-gradient()` or `radial-gradient()` written
        as in CSS. How gradients blend is set out in @{Layout}.

        A path is resolved the same way as an import path. It is relative to
        the file it is written in, or measured from the project's source root
        when it starts with `/`. It must point at a local file; remote URLs
        are not accepted, because the SVG and PNG renderers can't fetch them.
        The same rules apply to the `src` attribute of an image instance.

        Image files may be PNG, JPEG, WebP or SVG.

    keywords:
        Keywords, such as `flex`, `center` or `nowrap`, are written exactly as
        listed in each property's entry. They are case-sensitive.
